# Chapter 8. Pascal

The machine has two Pascals, and they share nothing but the language. Keep
them apart in your head from the start:

```
                   Turbo Pascal 3                       Mad Pascal
What it is         a program of 1985                    a cross-compiler of
                                                        today
Where it runs      on the machine: the Z80, under CP/M  on your desktop
What it makes      a CP/M .COM, for the Z80             a K/OS .prg, for the
                                                        45GS10
What it can touch  CP/M's world only                    every chip in the
                                                        machine
How you get it     you supply it; it is Borland's       in the repository,
                                                        free
```

Nothing carries over between them -- not the source, not the toolchain, not
the programs they produce. The first section is one, the second is the other.

## Turbo Pascal 3, on CP/M

Borland's Turbo Pascal 3 is a CP/M program, so it runs where CP/M runs: on the
Tube's Z80, in its own world, exactly as it did on a Kaypro. You write,
compile and run without leaving it, and what it produces is a CP/M program
that runs under CP/M on the Z80 -- it cannot see VICKY, the OPL2 or anything
else on the K4510 side of the Tube, any more than a Kaypro could.

It is not shipped, because it is Borland's. Put TURBO.COM and TURBO.MSG into
fs/CPM/H/3 -- the folder that CP/M's drive P: is a shortcut to -- and install
it for a "VT100" or "ANSI" terminal, which is what JIM is. Then, from the K/OS
prompt:

```
TURBO
```

TURBO is an alias that /SYSTEM/ETC/STARTUP.SAMPLE defines, for CPM K-TURBO:
boot CP/M, go to P:, start Turbo, and -- because the submit ends in EXIT --
come all the way back to the K/OS prompt when you leave it.

=> IMG/TURBO.PIC Turbo Pascal 3 starting on the Z80, its terminal drawn by JIM

Turbo Pascal 3 starting on the Z80, its terminal drawn by JIM.

Inside, it is 1985. The editor is WordStar's: Ctrl-E, S, D and X move (the
arrow keys are sent as those, so they work too; Home, End, PgUp and PgDn are
not, and do not), and Ctrl-K then D leaves the editor for the menu. R runs the
program in memory; O then C makes it compile to a .COM you can run from the
CP/M prompt by name.

## Mad Pascal, the cross-compiler

Mad Pascal is the other kind entirely -- a cross-compiler, like the cc65 this
machine's ROM is built with. You write on the desktop, mp compiles to 6502
assembly, MADS assembles it, and the result is a .prg beside the source in
/LANG/PASCAL that the shell's RUN loads like any other program -- and, since
2026-09-08, one the machine can make for itself: PAS HELLO compiles HELLO.PAS
in the directory you are standing in (Chapter 11, The Linux Underneath). It is
Tomasz Biela's compiler (MIT), a Turbo-Pascal-flavoured language with 8-, 16-
and 32-bit integers, fixed and floating point, strings, records, pointers,
units and inline assembly, made for the Atari and since taught the C64, the
X16 and the Neo6502. The K4510 is its newest target.

=> 13-LINUX.GMI Chapter 11, The Linux Underneath

```
PMANDEL
```

=> IMG/PMANDEL.PIC PMANDEL

PMANDEL: the Mandelbrot set in text, sixteen colours through JIM, in 250
frames.

That is demo/pas/pmandel.pas: the Mandelbrot set, 78 by 28 cells in the
machine's sixteen colours, in a little over four seconds at the desktop's 40.5
MHz -- fixed point, 32-bit integers, forty lines of Pascal. PSIEVE is the
classic sieve, ten passes timed by the frame counter; HELLO prints, shows the
colours, and echoes what you typed after its name. All of them are already
compiled and sitting beside their sources in /LANG/PASCAL, so you can run them
without installing anything.

### Building

On the desktop, with FPC and a Mad-Pascal checkout (the installer patches its
target table and rebuilds mp). It looks for the checkout in
/Projects/K4510/toolchain/Mad-Pascal unless MP_DIR says otherwise:

```
make pascal-install    # once; MP_DIR=... to point elsewhere
make pascal            # fs/LANG/PASCAL/*.PAS -> *.prg beside them
```

A new program is a file in fs/LANG/PASCAL; make finds it, and so does PAS on
the machine itself. The compiled .prg files are committed, so a machine
without the toolchain still runs them, and the test suite (test/pastest.sh)
runs HELLO and PSIEVE through the ROM.

### The target

Everything a Pascal program needs from the machine is in pascal/ of the
repository, laid out as it lives inside a Mad-Pascal checkout: the runtime
base (base/rtl6502_k4510.asm and base/k4510/), where @putchar writes to JIM,
the terminal (Chapter 5, The Tube); the SYSTEM and CRT units' machine halves
(lib/*_k4510.inc); and a k4510 unit. The consequences for the programmer:

=> 06-TUBE.GMI Chapter 5, The Tube

*

 Write and WriteLn go through JIM, so the CRT unit is the real thing: GotoXY,
TextColor and TextBackground (the palette's constants: BLUE, YELLOW,
LIGHT_GREEN...), ClrScr, ClrEol, InsLine/DelLine, WhereX/WhereY,
CursorOn/CursorOff, ReadKey and KeyPressed on the keyboard device, Delay and
Pause on the frame counter, and Sound on the machine's sound sequencer --
which changed its contract when the OPL2 became the machine's chip: Sound
takes a channel and a pitch in quarter-semitones and holds the note until
NoSound. TextMode(0) puts the ROM's screen back.
*

 ParamCount and ParamStr come from the shell line (RUN HELLO one two, or just
HELLO one two); ParamStr(0) is the whole tail.
*

 uses k4510 gives every chip as a typed variable at its address -- VICKY[reg],
DMA_SRC, FS_CMD, SYS_FRAMES, MATH_F[], TERM_CX and the rest -- plus
FarPeek/FarPoke into the 256 MB through the 45GS02's flat addressing,
DmaCopy/DmaFill, Shell('DIR'), LoadFile/SaveFile through the ROM, WaitVBlank,
and TermWrite for raw escape sequences -- each described under The k4510 unit,
below.
*

 Memory: code from $0800, the program's own; zero page $22-$3F for the
compiler's registers and $64-$A3 for its expression stack (the ROM keeps
$02-$21 and $F0-$F9); $0300 the string buffer. Programs return to the shell
with the ROM's state intact.

### Floating point on the MATH unit

single (IEEE-754, 32-bit) does not run in software here. The runtime's add,
subtract, multiply, divide, compare, Trunc, Round and Frac are the MATH unit
at $D700 (Chapter 13, The I/O Page): each is a few register moves and one
write to FOP, and the unit answers in the next cycle. PFLOAT times five
thousand rounds of multiply, divide, add and subtract: 5 frames on the unit,
24 with the software library (assemble with mads -d:SOFTFLOAT=1 to get it back
for comparison). The transcendentals are in the k4510 unit -- MathSqrt,
MathSin, MathCos, MathTan, MathAtan, MathAtan2, MathExp, MathLn, MathPow,
MathFloor -- one register write each. SYSTEM's own Sqrt/Sin/Cos/ArcTan/Exp/Ln
on single run on the unit too: the installer patches system.pas under {$ifdef
k4510}.

=> 21-IO.GMI Chapter 13, The I/O Page

### Graphics on VICKY

uses graph draws on VICKY's layer 1: a 640x480 8-bit bitmap at $200000 -- the
one BBC BASIC's ULA uses too -- over the console, which stays visible wherever
the picture is transparent (colour 0), as in a BBC MODE. InitGraph(0) switches
the video to 480 lines and clears the bitmap; CloseGraph puts the console's
mode back. PutPixel and GetPixel go through the 45GS02's flat addressing (the
MEGA65 multiplier does the row arithmetic); Line, LineTo and the K4510
additions DrawLine, FillBar and FillTriangle are the blitter's LINE, FILL and
TRIANGLE operations -- one register write each; Circle, Ellipse, Rectangle and
the rest of Mad Pascal's generic GRAPH build on those; OutTextXY writes with
the console's font. PGRAPH is the demonstration.

=> IMG/PGRAPH.PIC PGRAPH

PGRAPH: bars, a star and triangles by the blitter, circles by pixel, text from
the ROM's font -- and the console's own line still on top.

### The k4510 unit

uses k4510 is the machine itself, for a program that wants more than CRT and
GRAPH give it. Most of the unit is the chips' registers as variables at their
addresses (below); the rest is ten routines for memory, files and the shell,
and ten for the MATH unit. KUNIT in /LANG/PASCAL runs each of them once, and
is the source of the examples here.

##### Far addresses.

The unit's addresses are cardinals: physical addresses in the 256 MB, $0000000
to $FFFFFFF. The bottom 64 KB of it is the CPU's own view, so cardinal(@buf)
is the far address of a Pascal variable, and the DMA routines move between the
two. While a program runs, the far memory from $400000 to $FCFFFFF -- some 249
MB -- is its own. Below that the machine keeps the fonts ($10000) and the
console's screen ($30000), and GRAPH draws at $200000; above it are the ROM,
the sideways ROMs and the shell's own buffers.

FarPeek(a: cardinal): byte -- far memory
The byte at far address a, by the 45GS02's flat addressing.

FarPoke(a: cardinal; v: byte) -- far memory
Writes v there.

DmaCopy(src, dst, len: cardinal) -- far memory
Copies len bytes; overlapping is safe. The DMA engine has finished before the
call returns.

DmaFill(dst, len: cardinal; v: byte) -- far memory
Fills len bytes with v.

LoadFile(const name: string; dest: cardinal): cardinal -- files
Reads the whole file to far address dest and returns its length; 0 if it could
not (no such file -- or an empty one).

SaveFile(const name: string; src, len: cardinal): boolean -- files
Writes len bytes from src to the file, replacing it; true if it worked.

Shell(const cmd: string) -- the shell
Runs one line as if typed at the prompt: DIR, CD, COPY, MKDIR, ECHO and the
rest. Its output goes to the screen; the result is the byte at $03FF (Chapter
12, Memory), 0 if the command worked -- read it straight after: it is the last
byte of the runtime's string buffer, and the next string operation may
overwrite it.

=> 20-MEMORY.GMI Chapter 12, Memory

WaitVBlank -- timing
Waits for the next frame, a sixtieth of a second. SYS_FRAMES is the frame
count's low byte, for timing.

TermWrite(const s: string) -- the terminal
Sends s to JIM unchanged, VT100 escape sequences and all.

MathSqrt, MathSin, MathCos, MathTan, MathAtan, MathExp, MathLn, MathFloor (x:
single): single -- MATH unit
One write to the unit each; angles in radians, MathLn the natural log.

MathAtan2(y, x: single): single -- MATH unit
The angle of the point (x, y), - to .

MathPow(x, y: single): single -- MATH unit
x^y.

Names and command lines are cut at 95 characters, the length of the shell's
own line; a name is the shell's too, relative to the current directory or from
/.

```
uses k4510;
const FAR0 = $400000;
var buf: array[0..255] of byte;
    RC: byte absolute $03FF;            { the shell's result }
    len: cardinal;
begin
  DmaFill(FAR0, 256, 0);
  FarPoke(FAR0 + 7, 99);
  DmaCopy(FAR0, cardinal(@buf), 256);   { far -> buf: buf[7] = 99 }
  if SaveFile('/HOME/DATA.BIN', FAR0, 256) then
    len := LoadFile('/HOME/DATA.BIN', FAR0 + $1000);   { 256 }
  Shell('CD /NO-SUCH-DIR');             { RC <> 0: it failed }
end.
```

##### The registers.

Every chip is a variable at its address, typed to fit, under the names of
Appendix A, The Registers: VICKY[] (the 256 bytes at $D000), the keyboard (KBD
pops the next key, KBDST says whether one is waiting and which modifiers are
down), DMA_SRC/DST/LEN/CMD, the file system's FS_CMD and its friends (with the
FS_OPEN_READ... command constants), SYS[] and SYS_FRAMES, the MATH unit's
MATH_F[0..7] and MULTINA/MULTINB/MULTOUT, TUBE[], NET[], and JIM's TERM,
TERM_CX, TERM_CY, TERM_FG, TERM_BG and the rest. Reading or writing one is a
single load or store.

=> A1-REGISTERS.GMI Appendix A, The Registers

##### A bank window.

FarPeek is a call per byte. For a block of far memory used as an ordinary
array -- indexed, at full speed -- point an 8 KB block of the CPU's view at it
with a bank register (Chapter 12, Memory) and declare the array absolute
there. The unit has no names for the bank registers; declare the one you use:

=> 20-MEMORY.GMI Chapter 12, Memory

```
var win: array[0..8191] of byte absolute $8000;   { block 4 }
    BANK4: cardinal absolute D610;D600 + 4*4 }
    BANK4HI: byte absolute $D613;
begin
  BANK4 := 400000;       open: win[] is400000-$401FFF }
  win[100] := 1;         { lands in far memory }
  BANK4HI := FF;         shut:8000 is the program's RAM again }
end.
```

Writing the whole cardinal switches the block on as well (its top byte has bit
7 clear); $FF in that byte switches it off. Block n is $2000x n to $2000x n
+$1FFF. Use one the program does not occupy -- its code and variables run up
from $0800 (BANKWIN's end at $137A, so blocks 1 to 4 are free) -- and never 0
(zero page and stack) or 5 to 7, which the ROM banks in and out around every
system call. BANKWIN is the full demonstration, timed against FarPeek: the
window sums 64 KB in 8 frames, FarPeek in 17.

##### The rules.

*

 Code stays in the 64 KB; data does not have to. A program is at most
$0800-$CFFF, 50 KB with its variables; everything else goes in far memory.
*

 Do not Shell a program. RUN loads the other program over yours (Pascal
programs load at $0800, C programs at $6000) and there is nothing to come back
to. The shell's own commands are fine.
*

 Inline assembly that borrows :bp must put its low byte back to 0. Mad Pascal
indexes any array over 256 bytes through (:bp),y and counts on it; the unit's
own routines restore it.

## The library reference

What follows is every routine the K4510 target adds to Mad Pascal, unit by
unit, with what it does and a small example. (The k4510 unit, which reaches
the machine's memory, files and registers, is described above, under The k4510
unit.) Where a routine is Mad Pascal's own and merely runs on this machine
(WriteLn, Inc...) it is not repeated; Mad Pascal's own documentation has
those.

Every example in this section is untested. They were written from the units'
source, not compiled and run: the Mad Pascal toolchain was not to hand when
this section was written. Each one is a few lines that should compile as
shown; if one does not, the source in pascal/mp/lib is the authority and the
example is wrong.

A program names the units it wants on its uses line. crt, graph and k4510 are
always there to name. The bbc unit is a program in /LANG/PASCAL/EX (BBC.PAS);
to use it, keep a copy of BBC.PAS beside your own program, because PAS finds
units in the directory it compiles in.

### CRT: the screen, the keyboard, the clock and the sound

uses crt. Everything is drawn through JIM, the terminal (Chapter 5, The Tube),
so the screen is the console's own 80 columns by 30 rows. Columns and rows
count from 1, as in Turbo Pascal.

=> 06-TUBE.GMI Chapter 5, The Tube

##### ClrScr

Clears the screen and puts the cursor at the top left.

##### GotoXY(x, y: byte)

Moves the cursor to column x, row y. The next Write lands there.

##### WhereX: byte, WhereY: byte

The cursor's column and row.

##### TextColor(c: byte), TextBackground(c: byte)

The colour of the letters, and of the cell behind them, for everything written
afterwards. c is a palette index 0 to 15. The names are constants: BLACK,
WHITE, RED, CYAN, PURPLE, GREEN, BLUE, YELLOW, ORANGE, BROWN, LIGHT_RED,
DARK_GREY, GREY, LIGHT_GREEN, LIGHT_BLUE and LIGHT_GREY -- the Commodore 64's
sixteen.

##### ClrEol

Clears from the cursor to the end of the line.

##### InsLine, DelLine

Insert an empty line at the cursor's row, pushing the rest down; delete the
cursor's row, pulling the rest up.

##### CursorOn, CursorOff

Show or hide the cursor.

##### KeyPressed: Boolean

True when a key is waiting. It does not take the key.

##### ReadKey: char

The next key, waiting for one if there is none. Arrow and function keys come
as the machine's key codes, not as Turbo Pascal's two-character sequences.

##### Delay(ms: word)

Waits about ms milliseconds. The clock is the frame counter, so the wait is
rounded to frames of 16.7 ms.

##### TextMode(mode: byte)

Puts the ROM's screen and palette back, and resets JIM. mode is ignored;
TextMode(0) is the spelling.

##### Sound(chan, freq, dist, vol: byte)

Starts a note on the sound sequencer, which plays it on the OPL2. chan is 0 to
2 for the three tone channels, 3 for noise. freq is the pitch in quarter
semitones: 53 is middle C and every semitone is 4 more. dist is ignored (it is
there for Turbo Pascal's signature). vol is 0 to 15, the BBC's SOUND amplitude
without its minus sign: 15 is loudest. The note holds until NoSound or the
next Sound on that channel.

##### NoSound

Silences every channel.

Example (untested): a coloured menu, a key, and a note.

```
program crtdemo;
uses crt;
begin
  ClrScr;
  TextColor(YELLOW); TextBackground(BLUE);
  GotoXY(10, 5);  Write(' K4510 menu ');
  TextColor(WHITE); TextBackground(BLACK);
  GotoXY(10, 7);  Write('1  play a note');
  GotoXY(10, 8);  Write('Q  quit');
  repeat
    case UpCase(ReadKey) of
      '1': begin
             Sound(0, 53, 0, 12);   { middle C on tone channel 0 }
             Delay(500);
             NoSound;
           end;
    end;
  until False;   { Q is handled below in a real program }
end.
```

### SYSTEM: time, chance and the command line

These are the machine's halves of Mad Pascal's own SYSTEM unit, so they need
no uses.

##### Pause, Pause(n: word)

Wait one frame, or n frames (a sixtieth of a second each). Unlike Delay it
asks the frame counter directly, so Pause(60) is a second exactly.

##### Randomize

Seeds the generator from the clock.

##### Random(range: byte): byte, Random(range: smallint): smallint

A random number from 0 up to range - 1.

##### Random: Real, RandomF: Float

A random fraction, at least 0 and below 1, as a fixed-point Real or a single.

##### ParamCount: byte, ParamStr(i: byte): string

The words after the program's name on the shell line. ParamStr(1) is the
first; ParamStr(0) is the whole tail, spaces and all.

Example (untested): DICE 6 3 rolls three six-sided dice.

```
program dice;
var sides, n, i: byte;
begin
  Randomize;
  sides := 6; n := 1;
  if ParamCount >= 1 then sides := StrToInt(ParamStr(1));
  if ParamCount >= 2 then n := StrToInt(ParamStr(2));
  for i := 1 to n do Write(Random(sides) + 1, ' ');
  WriteLn;
end.
```

### GRAPH: lines, bars and text on VICKY

uses graph. A 640 by 480 picture, one byte a pixel, on VICKY's layer 1 over
the console. Pixels count from 0, (0,0) is the top left, and colour 0 is
transparent, so the console's text shows through wherever nothing is drawn.
Colours are palette indexes; 0 to 15 are the same sixteen as CRT's. Anything
off the screen is quietly dropped.

##### InitGraph(0)

Switches the video to 480 lines, points the layer at the bitmap and clears it.
Call it first. (InitGraph(driver, mode, path) is accepted too, for programs
written for other Mad Pascal targets; it does the same.)

##### CloseGraph

Switches the picture off and puts the console's video mode back. A program
that does not call it leaves the picture up after it ends.

##### ClearDevice

Clears the whole bitmap to transparent, by the DMA engine. Instant.

##### SetColor(c: byte)

The colour of everything drawn next.

##### SetBkColor(c: byte)

The ground colour VICKY shows where neither the picture nor the text has
anything.

##### PutPixel(x, y), PutPixel(x, y, c)

One pixel (x, y are smallint, c a byte), in the current colour or the colour
given.

##### GetPixel(x, y: smallint): byte

The colour of a pixel; 0 when it is off the screen.

##### DrawLine(x0, y0, x1, y1: smallint)

A line between two points. The blitter draws it: one register write each, with
no loop in Pascal.

##### LineTo(x, y: smallint), LineRel(dx, dy: smallint)

A line from the current position (which starts at 0,0 and is wherever the last
line ended) to a point, or by a distance.

##### FillBar(x0, y0, x1, y1: smallint)

A filled rectangle by the blitter. The corners may be in any order, and a bar
that hangs off the edge is clipped.

##### FillTriangle(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2: smallint)

A filled triangle by the blitter.

##### OutTextXY(x, y: smallint; s: string)

Draws s into the picture with the console's 8 by 8 font, its top left at x,y,
in the current colour.

Mad Pascal's own generic routines -- Circle, Ellipse, Rectangle, MoveTo and
the rest -- are built on these and work as they do on the other targets.

Example (untested): a frame, a star and a caption.

```
program gdemo;
uses crt, graph;
var i: byte;
begin
  InitGraph(0);
  SetColor(BLUE);   FillBar(40, 40, 600, 440);
  SetColor(YELLOW); FillTriangle(320, 80, 220, 300, 420, 300);
  SetColor(WHITE);
  for i := 0 to 15 do DrawLine(320, 240, 40 + i * 35, 440);
  OutTextXY(240, 450, 'HELLO, VICKY');
  ReadKey;
  CloseGraph;
end.
```

### BBC: the Tube ULA's drawing and sound, in Pascal

uses bbc. A port of BBC BASIC's graphics and sound words, so that a Pascal
program draws and sounds as its .BBC original does. It sits on GRAPH and keeps
the BBC's own world: coordinates run 1280 by 1024 from the bottom left, and
are scaled onto the 640 by 480 bitmap; logical colours are the BBC's, in
palette entries 16 to 31; sound is the sequencer with the BBC's units. Every
routine is the BBC word with a B in front. The eleven ports in /LANG/PASCAL/EX
are all written with it.

##### BMode(n: byte)

MODE n. 1 and 5 give four colours, 2 gives sixteen, any other graphics mode
two. 3, 6 and 7 put the bitmap away and the console back. It clears the screen
and sets the mode's default colours and GCOL.

##### BGcol(c: byte)

GCOL 0,c: the colour drawn with, c below 128; 128 and up sets the background
that BClg clears to.

##### BPalette(l, p: byte), BPaletteRGB(l, r, g, b: byte)

VDU 19: logical colour l shows physical colour p (0 to 7, the bits are red,
green, blue), or the exact red, green and blue given.

##### BOrigin(x, y: smallint)

ORIGIN x,y.

##### BMove(x, y: smallint), BDraw(x, y: smallint)

MOVE and DRAW: put the graphics cursor somewhere without drawing, or draw a
line to a point from the cursor.

##### BPlot(k: byte; x, y: smallint)

PLOT k,x,y. Of its many kinds this one does 4 and 5 (move and draw), 69 (a
point), 85 (a triangle with the two points before), 101 (a rectangle with the
point before) and 237 (a sprite).

##### BClg

CLG: the bitmap filled with the background.

##### BCircle(x, y, r: smallint), BCircleFill(x, y, r: smallint)

CIRCLE and CIRCLE FILL, centre and radius in BBC units.

##### Sprites

The machine has 128 hardware sprites; VICKY moves them, so a program draws
once and moves for nothing. BSpriteCapture(n, w, h) makes sprite n from the w
by h pixels (8, 16, 32 or 64) whose bottom left is at the graphics cursor (VDU
23,27,1). BSpriteSelect(n) chooses the sprite PLOT 237 will move.
BSpritePlot(x, y) places the selected sprite with its bottom left at x,y.
BSpriteShow(n, m) makes sprite n show sprite m's picture, so one picture
serves many sprites. BSpriteFlip(n, f) flips it (bit 0 across, bit 1 up and
down), BSpriteHide(n) hides it, and BSpritesOff turns the layer off.

##### BSound(ch, amp, pitch, dur)

SOUND ch,amp,pitch,dur (amp is a shortint, the rest byte). ch is 0 for noise,
1 to 3 for the tones. amp is 0 down to -15. pitch is in quarter semitones, 53
being middle C. dur is in twentieths of a second, and 255 holds the note. The
notes are queued: the sequencer keeps 64 a channel, so a whole tune can be
sent at once and it plays while the program goes on or ends. BSoundOff
silences every channel and empties the queues.

##### BWait(cs: word)

WAIT, in centiseconds, rounded to frames.

##### BRnd(n: word): word, BRandomize

RND(n): 1 to n, from a 32-bit generator -- Mad Pascal's own Random is a 16-bit
one whose pairs fall on a lattice, which the lines of KALEID showed up.
BRandomize seeds it from the clock.

##### BSx(x), BSy(y)

A BBC coordinate as a bitmap column or row, for a program that wants to mix
BDraw with GRAPH's PutPixel.

Example (untested, except for the sound, which is TUNE's): a mode 2 picture
with a held chord.

```
program bbcdemo;
uses crt, k4510, bbc;
begin
  BMode(2);                         { sixteen colours }
  BGcol(1);  BCircleFill(640, 512, 300);
  BGcol(7);  BMove(100, 100); BDraw(1180, 924);
  BPaletteRGB(8, 255, 128, 0);      { colour 8 is orange }
  BGcol(8);  BPlot(101, 900, 200);  { a rectangle from the cursor }
  BSound(1, -12, 53, 255);          { C, held }
  BSound(2, -12, 69, 255);          { an octave above, held }
  ReadKey;
  BSoundOff;
  BMode(7);                         { the console back }
end.
```

### Things this Mad Pascal does differently

Found while writing the demos; each cost an hour once.

*

 Result holds a function's value, and Exit(value) sets it and leaves at once
-- the BBC's = value. FUNCS.PAS is the example.
*

 A function's local variables, Result included, are static: one copy, shared
by every call. So Join(Join(a, b), c) gives the outer call the inner one's
Result. Call them one after the other into variables.
*

 Strings are joined with Concat(a, b), two at a time. + on strings is a syntax
error with a literal first and, with variables, compiles to an address sum.
*

 Pascal ignores case, so a variable called page and a constant called PAGE are
the same thing.
*

 A local array's own name is a pointer to it. To hand its address to a routine
in assembly, write adr.buf, not buf.

